> Nethack is written with an alphabet-style written system in mind,
> so how well it would translate into Chinese I can't guess.  It
> might be easier to port to Arabic than Mandarin.

Not sure... Chinese are more used to having nouns (animals, things,
etc) represented by single text characters on screen.  Where the
english version has "D" the chinese might have "=E9=BE=99" - the character
for dragon.  I see the kids telnetting to BBS systems in chinese
characters so there must be a way to implement a fixed-size fixed-font
window for that character set.

When I thought about it, I figured the hardest thing would be thinking
of good ways in Chinese to express "fire elemental" and "arch-lich" and
"astral plane" and other concepts that come from AD&D rather than from
folklore, books, and movies that our cultures share.  Also I was
thinking that I wouldn't be able to translate some item names without
giving away little spoilers: for instance I might use "waterproof sack"
instead of "oilskin sack".

Oh, that, and I'd have to refresh myself on C.  I'm mostly a web
programmer, I use foo-foo languages like PHP and VBScript, haven't used
C since freshman year of college.

It occurs to me I'd also want to change the control keys.



